# Spectra — Debug and automation API

Spectra ships a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code, without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time. It
is what you use to iterate on the combat, reproduce a specific dive or flip,
script a scenario, and capture clean screenshots of an exact
game state. This file defines that surface. Implement all of it, on the same
footing as the game itself.

Nothing here changes how a person plays. The debug API is inert during normal
play, doing nothing until something calls it, and the debug overlay is off until
toggled.

## A deterministic core

The whole surface rests on the simulation being deterministic and steppable, which
the simulation in `specs/controls.md` already requires: a fixed `120 Hz` timestep,
integrated in whole steps, decoupled from rendering. Two more properties make it
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances by stepping the simulation and must not
  depend on a canvas, on `requestAnimationFrame`, or on wall-clock time to make
  progress. Rendering reads the state, never the other way around.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses, such as which drone dives next,
  the dive gap, or a Flux's starting phase, runs off a seedable generator, so
  reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of API calls and steps, the game reaches
the same state every time.

## The manual step clock

So a scripted scenario is exact and reproducible regardless of machine load, the
simulation advances on an external timestep, and the game tracks who supplies it:

- The game holds an `autoStep` flag, `true` by default (ordinary play). While
  `autoStep` is `true`, the animation-frame loop advances the simulation from the
  wall clock, exactly as a player experiences it.
- `reset()` and `step()` set `autoStep` to `false`, beginning a driver-clocked
  session. While `autoStep` is `false`, the animation-frame loop still renders
  every frame but does not advance the simulation; `step(ticks)` is then the only
  thing that advances it, so a stepped scenario is exact and no stray wall-clock
  frame can pollute a measurement.
- `setAutoStep(enabled)` switches back and forth: `setAutoStep(true)` lets the game
  advance itself in real time again (useful for watching or recording a motion
  clip), and `setAutoStep(false)` returns to manual stepping. A `reset()` re-arms
  manual stepping. The input and other control operations below do not change
  `autoStep`.

This is an ordinary manual-step-versus-run-live debugging affordance: step the
world by hand to inspect it frame by frame, or let it run live to watch it.

## The `window.__spectra` object

Expose the API as a single object on the global `window.__spectra`, installed once
the game is running. It carries a `version` number (use `1`) and the operations
below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans so a caller can read them
directly; coordinates and velocities are in the logical-pixel space of
`specs/overview.md`, and a band is the string `"cyan"` or `"magenta"`.

### Core operations

- `reset(options)` returns the game to its initial title state and re-arms manual
  stepping. `options` is optional, and `options.seed` (a number) seeds all of the
  game's randomness so a scenario replays identically.
- `step(ticks)` advances the simulation by exactly `ticks` fixed steps
  immediately, running the fixed-timestep update internally rather than waiting for
  real frames, and switches to manual stepping. The unit is whole simulation ticks,
  not seconds: the timestep is `120 Hz` (`specs/controls.md`), so one tick is
  `1/120` of a second, `step(1)` runs a single fixed step and `step(120)` advances
  one second of game time. Nothing is rounded or approximated — the number of steps
  asked for is the number of steps run. `ticks` must be a non-negative integer;
  `step(0)` is legal and does nothing, while a fractional or negative value is
  invalid and the call fails loudly rather than guessing what was meant. This runs
  the real systems forward from a set-up state to see where they land. Stepping
  advances the live wave (drones, bullets, the ship, the discharge, and the timed
  state holds); it has no effect on a static menu screen.
- `snapshot()` returns a plain, JSON-serializable object describing the current
  game state (see [Snapshot shape](#snapshot-shape)). It is a pure read and never
  changes anything.
- `setAutoStep(enabled)` toggles the manual step clock described above.

### Control operations

These set up a specific situation. Each one routes through the same systems normal
play uses, arranging the world rather than faking outcomes.

- `startGame()` starts the mode this build defines (`specs/mode.md`) from the
  title, exactly as choosing it from the menu would, and enters its first stage's
  live wave (advancing past the stage-intro hold) so a caller lands in play ready
  to arrange a scenario.
- `startStage(stage)` begins a real stage numbered `stage` and enters its live wave
  directly, so a scenario can face a chosen stage's wave (its scaled speeds, its
  formation, or a challenge stage) without playing up to it. The wave itself is
  built by the real wave code for that stage.
- `setShipX(x)` places the ship's center at `x` along its lane (clamped to the
  legal range), as a precondition for aiming and collision scenarios.
- `setShipBand(band)` sets the ship's current band to `"cyan"` or `"magenta"`
  directly, as a precondition. This is the band the ship holds, with no fire
  lockout; use `flip()` when the flip's lockout is what matters.
- `flip()` performs a real band flip, exactly as the flip control does: the band
  changes instantly and the `0.30 s` fire lockout begins.
- `discharge()` triggers a real discharge, exactly as the discharge control does:
  it fires only if the resonance meter is full, spends the whole meter, and runs
  the real screen-clearing burst.
- `setResonance(value)` sets the resonance meter to `value` (`0` to `100`) as a
  precondition, so a scenario can arrange a ready or not-yet-ready discharge.
- `setLives(n)` sets the remaining lives, as a precondition.
- `setScore(n)` sets the score, as a precondition (for example just below an
  award threshold, so a real kill crosses it).
- `spawnDrone(spec)` places one drone into the live wave and returns its `id` (the
  stable id that appears in `snapshot()`). `spec` selects `kind` (`"shard"`,
  `"flux"`, or `"prism"`), `band` (`"cyan"` or `"magenta"`), a position `x`, `y`,
  and a `phase` (`"entering"`, `"formation"`, `"diving"`, or `"returning"`), and
  may also set `slotX` and `slotY` (the drone's resting slot), `shellBand` (for a
  Prism, the outer layer's band; the core is the opposite), and `fluxClock` (for a
  Flux, the seconds into its oscillation cycle, so a caller can place it in a known
  held or shimmer window). Spawning a drone arranges the world; the real systems
  then move, fire, and resolve it.
- `forceDive(id)` sends the drone with `id` from its slot into a real dive now,
  routing through the same dive-launch code the assault uses, so a scenario reaches
  a diver deterministically rather than waiting on the dive timer.
- `spawnPlayerBullet(spec)` places one of your bullets in flight as a precondition,
  with `spec` setting `x`, `y`, and `band`, and optionally `vx` and `vy` (defaulting
  to straight up at the player bullet speed). The real collision then decides what
  the shot does when it reaches a drone (destroy on a match, otherwise per
  `specs/mode.md`).
- `spawnEnemyBullet(spec)` places one enemy bullet in flight as a precondition,
  with `spec` setting `x`, `y`, and `band`, and optionally `vx` and `vy` (defaulting
  to straight down at the enemy bullet speed). The real shield then decides what it
  does when it reaches the ship (absorbed on a match, a lost life on a mismatch).
- `clearField()` removes every drone (formed, entering, diving, and pending) and
  every bullet from the wave, so a caller can pose an exact, uncluttered scenario.
  The stage, score, lives, resonance, and ship are untouched.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
- `setDroneCharge(id, charge)` sets the charge counter (`0` to `3`) of the drone
  with `id`, as a precondition, so a scenario can bring a drone to the brink and
  then let a real mismatched shot tip it into its overload reaction (`specs/mode.md`).
{{/if}}

A typical check calls `startGame()`, `clearField()`, then `spawnDrone` and either
`spawnPlayerBullet` (to test your fire against a drone) or `spawnEnemyBullet` (to
test the shield), `step()` a few ticks to run the real collision, and
reads the result from `snapshot()`.

### Input operations

The control operations above pose the world directly. The API can also inject
keyboard input, so a caller can drive the game the way a player does: navigate the
menus, start the mode from the title, move and fire, flip, discharge, pause, and
toggle mute. Injected input flows through the same handling the real keyboard
feeds, exercising the actual key bindings from `specs/controls.md` rather than a
parallel path. Injecting input does not switch the step clock: a held movement key
moves the ship through the game's normal play code when the simulation is stepped
or running live, so this is how a caller confirms the controls themselves work.

- `keyDown(code)` presses a key down. `code` is a standard `KeyboardEvent.code`
  (for example `"ArrowLeft"`, `"ArrowRight"`, `"KeyA"`, `"KeyD"`, `"Space"`,
  `"KeyW"`, `"KeyF"`, `"ShiftLeft"`, `"KeyX"`, `"Escape"`, `"KeyP"`, `"KeyM"`,
  `"Enter"`, `"ArrowUp"`, `"ArrowDown"`). The key becomes held, so a movement key
  drives the ship while it is held and the simulation advances, a held fire key
  auto-repeats at the fire cadence, and any one-shot action the key triggers on the
  current screen (a menu move, a confirm, a flip, a discharge, a pause, a mute
  toggle) is applied immediately.
- `keyUp(code)` releases a previously pressed key, ending its held state.
- `press(code)` is a convenience tap, a `keyDown` immediately followed by `keyUp`.
  This is the usual way to trigger a one-shot action without leaving the key held.

The usual shape for an input-driven scenario is to `press` through the menu to
start the mode, then `keyDown` a movement or fire key and `step` (or let real time
pass) so the ship moves or fires, then `keyUp` to release it, reading `snapshot()`
to see what happened.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns an object with at least these fields:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "stageIntro" | "inWave" | "paused" | "stageCleared" | "gameOver",
  menuIndex: <number>,           // the highlighted item on a menu screen
  mode: {{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}"overload"{{else}}"sortie"{{/if}},
  stage: <number>,               // the current stage number
  isChallenge: <boolean>,        // true on a challenge stage
  score: <number>,
  lives: <number>,
  resonance: <number>,           // 0..100
  dischargeReady: <boolean>,     // whether a discharge is available (meter full)
  muted: <boolean>,              // whether the mute toggle is currently on
  inversionActive: <boolean>,    // whether a spectral inversion is in effect
  ship: {
    x: <number>,                 // center x along the lane
    band: "cyan" | "magenta",    // the ship's current band
    alive: <boolean>,            // false during the READY hold after a hit
    canFire: <boolean>,          // false during the post-flip lockout or fire cooldown
    lockout: <number>,           // seconds of post-flip fire lockout remaining
  },
  discharge: { active: <boolean>, radius: <number> },  // the live discharge wave
  drones: [
    {
      id: <number>,              // stable id (spawnDrone / forceDive take this)
      kind: "shard" | "flux" | "prism",
      band: "cyan" | "magenta",  // the drone's stored band
      effectiveBand: "cyan" | "magenta", // what it currently reads/counts as
      x: <number>, y: <number>,
      phase: "entering" | "formation" | "diving" | "returning",
      slotX: <number>, slotY: <number>,  // its resting formation slot
      shimmer: <boolean>,        // flux: true during the 0.4s shimmer (no settled band)
      shellAlive: <boolean>,     // prism: whether the outer shell is intact
      shellBand: "cyan" | "magenta",     // prism: the shell layer's band
      coreBand: "cyan" | "magenta",      // prism: the core layer's band
{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}}
      charge: <number>,          // 0..3 charge toward an overload
{{/if}}
    },
  ],
  bullets: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, vx: <number>, vy: <number>,
      band: "cyan" | "magenta", effectiveBand: "cyan" | "magenta",
      friendly: <boolean> },     // true = your bullet (up), false = enemy (down)
  ],
  bursts: [
    { x: <number>, y: <number>, size: <number> },  // live drone-burst effects
  ],
  simTime: <number>,             // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

`effectiveBand` is the band an entity currently reads and counts as: normally the
same as its stored `band`, but swapped to the opposite while `inversionActive` is
true (`specs/drones.md`), so it reflects the field-wide swap. A player
bullet is never inverted. The `flux`-only `shimmer`, the `prism`-only `shell` and
`core` fields, and the ship's `lockout` and `canFire` report the mechanic
each drone or the ship is exercising.

## The debug overlay

Provide a read-only on-screen overlay showing the game's live internal state, so
you can watch what the simulation is doing while you play. It is toggled with the
backtick key (`` ` ``), off by default, and never changes gameplay; it only draws.

When on, it draws over the running game, legibly, in the game's monospace type, at
least: the current `screen`, `stage`, and `score`; the lives and resonance and
whether a discharge is ready; whether an inversion is active; the ship's `x`,
band, and remaining fire lockout; and for each live drone its `kind`, band (and
effective band under inversion), position, and phase, plus the Flux's shimmer
state and the Prism's shell/core state{{#if (eq variant.slug "overload")}} and its
charge{{/if}} — the same facts `snapshot()` reports. It is a diagnostic layer
rather than part of the game's presentation, so keep it visually plain and clearly
separate from the HUD.
